 75AS    76SE(D,3HE):XUNDL-2           2009KA06,2008KAZT                  200905
 75AS c  Includes {+76}Se(pol d,{+3}He)                                         
 75AS c  Compiled (unevaluated) dataset from 2009Ka06:                          
 75AS2c  Phys Rev C 79, 021301(R) (2009); and 2008KaZT: private communication   
 75AS3c  received from B.P. Kay et al., Argonne National Lab, Dec 2, 2008       
 75AS c  2008KaZT contains data details for eight different reactions.          
 75AS c  Compiled by B. Singh (McMaster): Dec 11, 2008.                         
 75AS c  Edited by B. Singh, Jan 14, 2009 in response to some                   
 75AS2c  corrected VAP values received from 2008KaZT                            
 75AS c  Edited by B. Singh, April 28, 2009 to include 2009Ka06 reference       
 75AS c  E=80 MeV beam provided by the AVF cyclotron at RCNP, Osaka. Enriched   
 75AS2c  target. Vector-polarized 80-MeV deuteron beam with A{-y}=0.520 {I10}.  
 75AS3c  {+3}He outgoing particles detected and analyzed with Grand Raiden      
 75AS4c  magnetic spectrograph, with an angular aperture of |+1.1|'. FWHM=50 keV
 75AS c  Measured precise absolute cross sections and relative cross sections   
 75AS2c  where these are maximum for the relevant L transfer, angular           
 75AS3c  distributions and polarization asymmetries. Vector analyzing powers    
 75AS4c  were deduced from the measured polarization asymmetries by dividing    
 75AS5c  by a factor of 1.04 (2*[beam vector polarization=0.520 {I10}]).        
 75AS6c  Spectroscopic factors were deduced from analysis of cross              
 75AS7c  section data by DWBA calculations using PTOLEMY code and six           
 75AS8c  different sets of optical-model potential parameters and two           
 75AS9c  bound-state potential parameters.                                      
 75AS c  Single-particle transfer reactions studied in 2008KaZT:                
 75AS2c  {+74}Ge(d,{+3}He){+73}Ga; {+76}Ge(d,{+3}He){+75}Ga;                    
 75AS3c  {+76}Se(d,{+3}He){+75}As; {+78}Se(d,{+3}He){+77}As;                    
 75AS4c  {+74}Ge({+3}He,d){+75}As; {+76}Ge({+3}He,d){+77}As;                    
 75AS5c  {+76}Se({+3}He,d){+77}Br; {+78}Se({+3}He,d){+79}Br.                    
 75AS6c  For (d,{+3}He) reactions, polarized deuteron beam is also used to      
 75AS7c  obtain asymmetries and subsequent information about parities of levels.
 75AS8c  See relevant dataset in XUNDL database for each reaction.              
 75AS c  The experiments were designed to map out the occupancies of valence    
 75AS2c  proton orbitals in the ground states of {+74}Ge, {+76}Ge, {+76}Se and  
 75AS3c  {+78}Se by precise measurements of absolute cross sections and relative
 75AS4c  cross sections at the angles where these are maximum in angular        
 75AS5c  distributions in single-particle transfer reactions.                   
 75AS c  Uncertainty in cross sections: statistical uncertainty of 1%           
 75AS2c  for strong peaks; systematic uncertainties of 5% in absolute values    
 75AS3c  and 3% in relative values. Multiplets have larger uncertainties        
 75AS c  ENSDF database available at: http://www.nndc.bnl.gov/ensdf/            
 75AS c  All data are from 2008KaZT unless otherwise stated                     
 75AS cL E,J$ Unless otherwise noted, 2008KaZT take values from 'adopted levels,
 75AS2cL gammas' dataset for {+75}As in the ENSDF database. A 618-keV group     
 75AS3cL reported in an earlier (d,{+3}He) work with |?10% intensity that of    
 75AS4cL the ground state cross section was not seen by 2008KaZT with a limit   
 75AS5cL |<2% of the ground state cross section                                 
 75AS cL E(X)$ 265, 280 and 304 groups are unresolved, angle-to-angle ratios of 
 75AS2cL cross sections used to assign separate cross sections. See details     
 75AS3cL of the fitting procedure for unresolved peaks in 2008KaZT              
 75AS cL E(Y)$ 572 and 585 groups are unresolved, angle-to-angle ratios of      
 75AS2cL cross sections used to assign separate cross sections. See details     
 75AS3cL of the fitting procedure for unresolved peaks in 2008KaZT              
 75AS cL E(Z)$ 1431 group corresponds to L=1+4 doublet as in earlier            
 75AS2cL literature (although L=1 was also proposed in another study, albeit    
 75AS3cL with a poor fit to angular distribution), angle-to-angle ratios of     
 75AS4cL cross sections used to assign separate cross sections. See details     
 75AS5cL of the fitting procedure for unresolved peaks in 2008KaZT              
 75AS cL J(B)$ From measured polarization asymmetry and deduced vector          
 75AS2cL analyzing powers in 2008KaZT                                           
 75AS cL L$ 2008KaZT relied on the assignment of L transfers                    
 75AS2cL in previous studies (see ENSDF database for {+75}As). These assignments
 75AS3cL were, nevertheless, confirmed in 2008KaZT from their measured          
 75AS4cL angle-to-angle ratios                                                  
 75AS CL S$LABEL=C2S                                                            
 75AS cL S$ From 2008KaZT. The spectroscopic factors were deduced for different 
 75AS2cL L transfers as follows: 4.5|' data for L=1, 8|' data for L=3, and 12|' 
 75AS3cL data for L=4. The values are the ratios of experimental cross sections 
 75AS4cL and calculated DWBA cross sections using the finite-range PTOLEMY code.
 75AS5cL The normalization procedure is described in 2008KaZT. Six different    
 75AS6cL sets of optical-model potential parameters and two different           
 75AS7cL bound-state potential parameters were used for the calculations. See   
 75AS8cL 2008KaZT for listing of these parameters. Differences in C{+2}S values 
 75AS9cL were less than 10% for different sets of optical parameters used.      
 75ASAcL The effects of the bound-state parameters were also within the         
 75ASBcL uncertainties.                                                         
